informal_proof:
To show that the matrix  
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
e^{i a} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & e^{i b} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & e^{i c}
\end{pmatrix}
\]  
is unitary, we need to verify that when we multiply the matrix by its conjugate transpose, we obtain the identity matrix.

The conjugate transpose of a matrix is obtained by taking the transpose and then taking the complex conjugate of each entry. Since our matrix is diagonal, taking the transpose does not change it, and the complex conjugate of \( e^{i a} \) is \( e^{-i a} \). Therefore, the conjugate transpose of our matrix is:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
e^{-i a} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & e^{-i b} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & e^{-i c}
\end{pmatrix}
\]  

Multiplying the original matrix by its conjugate transpose:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
e^{i a} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & e^{i b} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & e^{i c}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
e^{-i a} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & e^{-i b} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & e^{-i c}
\end{pmatrix}
\]  

yields:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
e^{i a} e^{-i a} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & e^{i b} e^{-i b} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & e^{i c} e^{-i c}
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\]  

Since this product is the identity matrix, the original matrix is indeed unitary.

Furthermore, each entry in the diagonal is a complex number of unit magnitude (\( |e^{i a}| = 1 \)), ensuring that the matrix preserves the inner product and, particularly, the norm of any vector it acts upon, which is essential in quantum computing.

Hence, the matrix is unitary.

